    "description": "Iran’s Persian-language e-book services combine digital catalogues with reading software and account-based libraries. Fidibo and Taaghche illustrate how access to a digital edition can depend on a platform’s applications, publisher agreements, and subscription conditions.",
    "tags": ["E-books", "Persian literature", "Digital publishing", "Reading platforms", "Digital access"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:56:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:56:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "E-books in Iran: Persian Reading Platforms and Digital Access",
      "description": "Explore Persian e-book platforms, account-based reading, publisher restrictions, offline access, and the limits of available market evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "digital-editions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A book and its reading environment",
        "content": "For readers exploring Persian books digitally, the reading service matters alongside the title. Fidibo’s catalogue separates electronic books from audiobooks, magazines, and podcasts; its site also provides links to mobile and desktop applications. Taaghche presents electronic books and audio within a service available across mobile devices, computers, and the web. These are examples of combined distribution and reading environments, rather than simply directories of downloadable documents. Their public descriptions establish the services they advertise, not their relative market shares or the size of Iran’s reading public. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction between a digital edition and a freely transferable file is especially useful. Taaghche’s help documentation says its publisher agreements restrict reading to its application and website. It describes encryption as a condition of delivering books under those agreements. This supports a specific account of Taaghche’s distribution model; it does not establish a universal rule for every electronic publication available in Iran. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "purchase-subscription-offline",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Purchase, subscription, and offline reading",
        "content": "A purchased book and a subscription selection can have different access conditions. Taaghche’s offline-reading guide says a purchased book can be read without an internet connection after it has been downloaded. The same guide describes a separate restriction for its subscription service, Taaghche Bi-Nahayat, or “Unlimited”: only the most recently obtained subscription book is available offline, while the subscription collection remains accessible online. That distinction makes the method of obtaining a title relevant to how it can later be read. [s4]\n\nOffline access should therefore be understood as a feature within the service, rather than evidence that the reader has received an unrestricted archive copy. A download can support reading without a continuous connection while remaining subject to the platform’s file and access arrangements. Taaghche’s download instructions and its explanation of publisher agreements describe these two aspects separately. Readers assessing a digital edition need to consider both. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpreting-market",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the available evidence establishes",
        "content": "The inspected catalogues demonstrate that Persian electronic books sit within a broader digital publishing offer, alongside listening and periodical products. They do not establish how many people finish books, which provinces are best served, or how access differs by income, disability, or language. A catalogue listing is evidence of an advertised offering; it is not a measure of readership. [s1][s2]\n\nThe most useful starting point is consequently the particular edition and its documented reading conditions. Platform descriptions explain where a book is offered, while help pages explain important limits on using it. This page’s evidence supports that practical distinction, but a fuller account of Iran’s e-book sector requires independent research on readership, accessibility, publisher economics, and developments over time.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
