    "description": "Persian audiobook platforms distribute spoken editions through account libraries, listening interfaces, and downloads. Their documentation reveals important distinctions between distribution and production, offline playback and file transfer, and individually purchased titles and subscription access.",
    "tags": ["Audiobooks", "Persian audio", "Narration", "Digital publishing", "Offline listening"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:56:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:56:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Audiobooks in Iran: Distribution, Downloads and Listening",
      "description": "Understand Persian audiobook distribution, publisher roles, offline playback, transferable files, and the limits of available sector evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "audio-ecosystem",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A listening catalogue contains different kinds of work",
        "content": "Persian digital listening services distinguish several products that should not automatically be treated as equivalent. Fidibo’s navigation separates audiobooks, podcasts, and audio serials. Taaghche’s catalogue also distinguishes audiobooks, book summaries, and serial audio. These labels matter when describing what is available: the presence of a spoken item does not by itself establish that it is a complete reading of a published book. Catalogue categories provide a starting point for identifying the product, rather than a substitute for checking the individual edition. [s1][s2]\n\nThe platform selling an audiobook is not necessarily the organization producing it. In its response to prospective narrators, Fidibo describes itself as an audiobook distributor and directs applicants to audiobook publishers. This documents Fidibo’s stated role in that help answer. It should not be generalized into a claim that all Iranian platforms follow the same production arrangements. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "downloads",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From purchase to offline playback",
        "content": "Taaghche’s listening guide describes a concrete sequence: after purchase, the audiobook appears in “My Books”; the listener opens the playback controls and chapter list, then downloads its sections. Offline listening becomes available after those downloads finish. The guide therefore makes a useful distinction between having a title in an account and having its audio available on the device. A library entry alone does not establish that every section has been downloaded. [s4]\n\nFile transfer is a separate question. Taaghche explains that some audiobooks carry a transferability designation, allowing direct access to their audio files. Others are encrypted at the publisher’s request and do not provide that direct access. Consequently, an audiobook’s availability for listening within a service should not be read as a promise that its files can be moved to another player. The documented arrangement depends on the title’s transferability status. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "subscription-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Access conditions and unanswered questions",
        "content": "Subscriptions introduce another distinction. Fidibo’s troubleshooting guidance says that a desired book must belong to the FidiPlus subscription selection, that the subscription must remain active, and that the subscriber must have remaining capacity to obtain books. Its answer also identifies an internet connection as a requirement of that subscription service. These conditions describe a provider’s access model; they are not evidence about how frequently subscribers listen or how much revenue publishers receive. [s3]\n\nTogether, the documentation supports a precise way of approaching Persian audiobooks: identify the kind of audio work, distinguish its publisher from its distributor, and check the conditions governing playback and file access. It leaves broader cultural and economic questions unresolved. No national listening trend, regional comparison, or assessment of accessibility can be inferred from these help pages alone.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
